# Git attributes for the Relay monorepo.
#
# This file has two jobs, in priority order.
#
#   1. Protect the read-only inputs. The reference frame corpus under
#      screenshots/ is 1,022 binary capture files that must remain
#      byte-identical, and the specification catalog, the vendor documentation
#      folder and the two root metadata files are read-only inputs alongside it.
#      An end-of-line normalisation is a re-encoding, so no normalising
#      attribute is ever declared over any of those paths.
#
#   2. Keep authored text and review diffs predictable. Normalisation is
#      declared tree by tree over the authored source only, and generated
#      artifacts are collapsed in review without being hidden from it.
#
# There is deliberately NO repository-wide `* text=auto` here. A global
# normalisation directive is the single most likely way to rewrite a byte in a
# tracked read-only file, and byte-identity is absolute. Normalisation is
# therefore opt-in per path, and the last block re-asserts "no conversion" over
# the read-only inputs so that nothing above it can reach them.
#
# Read this together with .dockerignore, which handles the other half of corpus
# containment: keeping the corpus out of every build context, container image
# and client bundle. .prettierignore excludes the same read-only paths from
# formatting runs.
#
# Every pattern that addresses the corpus matches on DIRECTORY and EXTENSION
# only. The corpus filenames carry a third party's product name and the corpus
# may not be renamed, so no filename form taken from it is ever written into
# authored source.
#
# Pattern syntax note: Git matches these patterns with the same rules as
# .gitignore. Brace alternation is NOT supported, so each extension gets its
# own line. A leading slash anchors a pattern to the repository root.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Reference frame corpus - read-only binary evidence, byte-identical
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `binary` is a macro for `-text -diff -merge`. All three are also stated
# explicitly so the guarantee is legible without expanding the macro:
#   -text   never convert line endings, in either direction, ever. This also
#           overrides any core.autocrlf or core.eol a contributor has set
#           globally, which is what makes the byte-identity guarantee hold on
#           any machine.
#   -diff   never attempt a textual diff over binary content.
#   -merge  never synthesise a merged result inside a read-only tree; a
#           conflict is reported instead. This affects merge results only and
#           leaves a normal checkout untouched.
screenshots/**                            binary -text -diff -merge
screenshots/**/*.png                      binary -text -diff -merge

# The corpus is vendored input, not project source: keep it out of repository
# language statistics.
screenshots/**                            linguist-vendored

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Binary asset types, wherever they appear
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These declarations only ever unset `text`, so they cannot trigger a
# conversion pass on anything.
*.png                                     binary
*.jpg                                     binary
*.jpeg                                    binary
*.gif                                     binary
*.webp                                    binary
*.avif                                    binary
*.ico                                     binary
*.woff                                    binary
*.woff2                                   binary
*.ttf                                     binary
*.otf                                     binary

# Vector icon artwork is XML, so it stays text and stays reviewable as a diff.
*.svg                                     text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Authored source trees - stored and checked out with LF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
apps/**/*.ts                              text eol=lf
apps/**/*.tsx                             text eol=lf
apps/**/*.css                             text eol=lf
apps/**/*.html                            text eol=lf
apps/**/*.json                            text eol=lf

packages/**/*.ts                          text eol=lf
packages/**/*.tsx                         text eol=lf
packages/**/*.css                         text eol=lf
packages/**/*.prisma                      text eol=lf
packages/**/*.sql                         text eol=lf
packages/**/*.json                        text eol=lf

tools/**/*.ts                             text eol=lf
tools/**/*.json                           text eol=lf
tools/**/*.md                             text eol=lf

e2e/**/*.ts                               text eol=lf
e2e/**/*.json                             text eol=lf

infra/**/*.yml                            text eol=lf
infra/**/*.yaml                           text eol=lf
infra/**/*.sql                            text eol=lf
infra/**/*.sh                             text eol=lf
infra/**/*.md                             text eol=lf

.github/**/*.yml                          text eol=lf
.github/**/*.yaml                         text eol=lf

# Authored documentation. docs/workflows/ is a read-only input and is
# deliberately excluded from this scope; see the last block.
docs/index.md                             text eol=lf
docs/decisions/**/*.md                    text eol=lf

# A shell script checked out with CRLF endings fails inside a Linux container
# with an obscure error, so the committed script is declared on its own as well
# as by the tree pattern above.
infra/minio/bootstrap.sh                  text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Root configuration and documentation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The leading slash anchors each pattern to the repository root, so none of
# these can reach into a tree further down.
/*.json                                   text eol=lf
/*.yaml                                   text eol=lf
/*.yml                                    text eol=lf
/*.md                                     text eol=lf
/*.js                                     text eol=lf
/*.cjs                                    text eol=lf
/*.mjs                                    text eol=lf
/*.ts                                     text eol=lf
/.nvmrc                                   text eol=lf
/.npmrc                                   text eol=lf
/.env.example                             text eol=lf
/.gitignore                               text eol=lf
/.gitattributes                           text eol=lf
/.dockerignore                            text eol=lf
/.prettierignore                          text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Generated artifacts - collapsed in review, never hidden from it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lockfile is committed on purpose: it is the record of exactly which
# versions this build resolves. Its diff is suppressed so that a routine
# dependency refresh cannot drown a review, and it is flagged generated so it
# does not count as authored source. `git diff --text` still shows it on
# demand.
/pnpm-lock.yaml                           text eol=lf -diff linguist-generated=true

# The API description is generated from the shared schemas and committed so
# that drift from the server is visible. Flagged generated, but its diff stays
# on: a contract change has to be readable in review.
packages/shared/src/openapi/openapi.json  text eol=lf linguist-generated=true

# Raw frame-measurement output is committed evidence that the design tokens
# were measured rather than estimated. It is deliberately NOT flagged
# generated, and `diff` is stated explicitly, because a reviewer must be able
# to read exactly what changed when a token value moves.
tools/measure-frames/out/*.json           text eol=lf diff

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Read-only inputs - no conversion, ever. Keep this block last.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These paths are inputs to the build and are never edited by it. `-text` is a
# no-op on content: it tells Git to store and check each file out byte for
# byte and to ignore any core.autocrlf or core.eol a contributor has set
# globally. It is declared last because a later line wins, so nothing above can
# reintroduce a normalising attribute over a path that must not be re-encoded.
# Two of these are also matched by the root patterns in block 4, which is
# exactly why this block comes after it.
#
# `diff` is deliberately left on for these paths. They must never change, and
# if one ever does, the change has to be readable in review. The frame corpus
# is the fifth read-only input and is handled in block 1, where `-diff` is
# correct because its content is binary.
docs/workflows/**                         -text
blitzy/documentation/**                   -text
/catalog-info.yaml                        -text
/mkdocs.yml                               -text
